A||||5||fiero56||15"x7" 's on the front of an '88||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||09-29-2001||06:34 PM||||I feel my Formula pushes the front end too much even with slightly modified suspension. I'm looking at putting 215/60/15 all around, but don't want to put 215 on a 6" wide rim. The 7" wide rim will stick out 1" farther from the car on each side, but it will allow me to put 215 tires on without looking bad. Has anyone done this to an '88, or have pictures? I might/could swap my current wheels to see how it looks, but living in an apt with no garage, I might/will look at little red-necky.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||09-29-2001||08:03 PM||||Don't do it.  Aside from looking awkward, your handling might also be adversely affected......the offsets are just too different (the 7" is 35mm and the 6' is 42mm).<P>215/60's will fit just fine on the 6" rim.....some tires will fit differently, but all in all it isn't such a big deal (just make sure to go with a larger size in the rear, like 225/60).   <P>I don't have my digital camera on me right now, but tomorrow I will snap some pics of the extra set of '88 GT rims I have.....they have 215/60's on all 4 wheels.  That'll give you an idea of how it'll look.<P>Another note.....a 215/60 tire is only 10mm "wider" than a 205 which is about .4 inches (less than half an inch).....the 6" rim is capable of handling it and it will still look 1000% better than 15x7's up front (just my opinion).<P><BR>I'll post those pics tomorrow.<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rick 88||09-29-2001||08:17 PM||||I agree with Mr Fiero. I have seen 88's where the wheels have been rotated by tire shops. You will not be happy with the look of the 7in. wheel on the front. There are some sites where you can plug in your stock tire size and compare it to different aspect ratios to match overall diameter while adding width. <P>The Falken tire site has some great charts with all the measurements, and rim size recommendations you will probably need. Also, some of the autocross guys can tell you what they are running on their 88 stock wheels.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wannabe||09-29-2001||09:03 PM||||I have the 7" wide rims with 215-60-15s on my  Formula. As far as I'm concerned they look dumb, but I originally had 225-55-15s on them that would not fit properly on the 6" rims. It's hard to compare the handling difference because the original rims had all season tires and the 7" rims have r-compounds on them. Of hand, I'd say that you won't see a big improvement in grip by putting a bigger tire on the 6" rims. 6" is technically less than ideal even for a 205 tire, although it's a common combination. If you like I can snap a pic and send it to you. for some reason I can't get pip to work.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jefrysuko||09-29-2001||10:49 PM||||I have 215/60 on the front and 225/60 on the rear of my GT and I like both the way it looks and preforms.  I was worried about having the tires extend past the rims and making the car feel unresponsive but that doesn't seam to be the case.  The wheel wells are filled just a little bit more which I think is why it looks better.  Here is a picture but I don't think it will show you much.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Zippy_11_001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Jefrysuko (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||ray b||09-30-2001||01:42 PM||||just gets some softer/stickyer tyres up front.<BR>fronts don't wareout very fast anyway.<BR>or try lower a/r 50 or 195/45/15 koko or yoko make them, lowers the car too<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
